<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Codelco</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1976–</span> · Also known as National Copper Corporation of Chile</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Codelco, the National <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">Copper</a> Corporation of Chile, is the state-owned company created in 1976 to manage the large copper mines nationalised in 1971, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chuquicamata">Chuquicamata</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-teniente">El Teniente</a>. It is the world's largest copper producer. A share of its export earnings long went directly to the armed forces under a law dating from 1958, which was repealed in 2019.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/codelco.jpg" alt="Codelco" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: zzebah_punk · (Hablemos) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Codelco_logo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
